            
            
            
                    Microsoft OS/2 Device Driver Development Kit
            
            
            
            These two  diskettes contain  the release  of the  Microsoft
            Operating System  / 2 Device Driver Kit (DDDK).  This kit is
            intended  to  be  used  in  conjunction  with  the  Software
            Development Kit for OS/2 1.0, however, it must be built with
            C 5.1  and Masm  5.1.  Note that the device drivers included
            with this kit are MS OS/2 1.0 device drivers only.
            
            The kernels provided with this release will only work on MS
            OS/2.
            
            The DDDK can be built in either the Real Mode Box of OS/2 or
            with DOS.   A global build batch file, build.bat, will build
            the contents  of the  DDDK when  it is  run.  The batch file
            must  be   run  from  the  named  DDDK  root  directory,  no
            parameters are used.
            
            The directory structure on the disks should be duplicated on
            your  development  system,  as  there  are  dependencies  in
            makefiles, and  linker response  files  that  rely  on  this
            structure.
            
            To set up the DDDK copy the setup.bat file from disk 1 to
            the drive and directory which is to be the root directory of
            the DDDK (ex. c:\dddk). Then run the batch file naming the
            source drive (i.e. setup a:).  Finally, run build.bat to
            create the DDDK binaries.
            
            Be sure  the tools  can be  accessed by the PATH environment
            variable.   Also, the LIB environment variable must point to
            the libraries  and   the INCLUDE  environment to  the header
            files.
            
            For example, the following should be in autoexec.bat:
            
                 PATH=c:\bin;c:\dddk\tools
                 INCLUDE=c:\include
                 LIB=c:\lib
            
            The example above assumes the DDDK was installed on c: under
            the root directory of dddk.
            
            The directory  called TEMPLATE  holds copies of all binaries
            created with  the DDDK  and the  version 5.1  tools.    This
            directory is  used to verify the build environment by binary
            comparing built  programs to  these programs.   The supplied
            batch file compare.bat, found in the template directory, can
            help do this.
            
            Since different  build tools  are used  to  build  the  disk
            device driver  included with the DDDK than are used to build
            the OS/2 1.0 in the December 1987 SDK the resulting binaries
            will differ.  There are also some fixes which are not in the
            December  1987   SDK  release   which  will  prevent  binary
            compares.
            
            The  diskdd   directory  contains   a  small   file   called
            disktest.asm.   This file does a disk read for a simple disk
            I/O test.
            
            There are some include files in some directories that have
            the same name as include files in other directories.  These
            include files are different versions of the same include
            file, and must not be used interchangeably.
            
            NOTES and ERRATA:
            
              1  In DDDK Guide section 3.2 (page 15), "Setting Up the
                 DOS- Resident Debugger", some additional notes are      
                 necessary. First, the bootable disk referred to in step
                 1 is a copy of the boot disk provided with the December
                 1987 SDK.
            
              2  The MAPSYM utility supplied with this kit will not run
                 in protect mode,  this will affect make files, when     
                 they are run in the protect mode by reporting mapsym    
                 fails for an unknown reason.
            
              3  In DDDK Guide section 3.7 (pp. 56-58) reference is made
                 to "Debugging with DBPRT Statements."  Please disregard
                 this section as DBPRT is not supported in this release.
            
              4  On page 66, Section 4.2.1, "Running the ADD Tests", the
                 message regarding switching displays is not supported
                 in this release.  All debug output will go to the debug
                 terminal.
            
              5  On page 105, step 8, type G SETUP_DEVICE (not           
                 SET_UP_DEVICE) and type P 2 before examining/modifying
                 the baud rate in AX.
            
              6  On page 105, step 10, G should be repeated to reach the
                 desired break point.
            
              7  On page 143, step 14, if carry is set after the call,   
                 type BP CS:IP and G until carry is not set before       
                 continuing.
            
              8  On page 16, the introduction messages have been updated
                 to   state the machine processor type, [80286] or       
                 [80386],  they will appear after the Microsoft          
                 copyright message.
            
              9  The debugger no longer stops at boot time, as a result
                 the examples in the user's guide require one less "G"
            
              10 The testing kernel, os2dost.com, is now called          
                 os2dosdd.com.

	      11 disktest.com is now disktest.exe






































